.Dd October 10, 2026
.Dt WATCH 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm watch
.Nd run a command repeatedly and show its output
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl bcdegptx
.Op Fl n Ar seconds
.Ar command Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl v | Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs
.Ar command
every two seconds and shows its output on the whole screen, with a header
that names the command, the interval and the time.
The command runs through
.Pa /bin/sh Fl c
with the words joined by spaces, or directly with
.Fl x .
Its standard output and standard error are shown together; lines are cut at
the width of the window.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n , \-interval Ar seconds
Seconds between runs; the least is 0.1.
.It Fl d , \-differences
Show the characters that changed since the last run in reverse video.
.It Fl t , \-no\-title
Leave out the header.
.It Fl e , \-errexit
Stop, after a key, when the command exits with a non-zero status (exit status 8).
.It Fl g , \-chgexit
Stop when the output changes.
.It Fl b , \-beep
Ring the bell when the command exits with a non-zero status.
.It Fl c , \-color
Pass the command's colour sequences through; other escape sequences are dropped.
.It Fl p , \-precise
Count the interval from the start of a run, not from its end.
.It Fl x , \-exec
Run the command directly, without a shell.
.It Fl v , \-version
Print the version.
.It Fl h , \-help
Print a short help text.
.El
.Pp
While it runs:
.Ql q
quits and a space runs the command again at once.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 after
.Ql q
or a signal, 1 on a usage error, 8 after
.Fl e
stopped it.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1
